            <div xml:id="d581">

                <pb facs="0881" n="779" sameAs="frus:frus1933v01/pg779"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">550.S1 Economic Commission/94: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gilbert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1933-10-06T12:00:00+01:00">October 6,
                            1933—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 12:45 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>218. 1. Colijn has returned to The Hague. Replies being collated by Stoppani who
                    will report to Colijn.</p>
                <p>2. Conveyed to Stoppani information given in the Department’s telegram 96,
                    October 5, 11 a.m., who informed me that Great Britain now considers that the
                    initiative should be left entirely to the producers and that Canada and Belgium
                    were taking the same position. Representatives of other countries are awaiting
                    instructions.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gilbert</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
